My verdict likely: not ready to be the judge of every build, because (a) its input contract does not read use cases or the owner's corrections, which R12.2 names; (b) it is calibrated on one product with one judged case, and cost ~1.24M tokens/20+ min per run; (c) it can output MEETS only; result states don't include "not applicable"/"could not run" (the memory-check lesson five states), though UNKNOWN approximates; (d) no wiring into guard, and verdict.json lacks product commit/engine/harness metadata for a R12.2 failure record; (e) branch not merged; workflow requires scripts at repo root, so not runnable from master; (f) known false-PASS hole F5(c) single-probe mutant run without `runs` key; (g) O8 open (PASS proof per probe vs at least one probe), and F5(c) or O8.

But need to check "can it ever report PASS without proof": code analysis shows executable/static PASS requires green on product + red on valid, healthy mutant. Judged PASS requires plant found + holds true + evidence string. But there is the known hole F5(c) (single probe run with no runs key) which is a proof hole only for old matrices; calibration K1d's matrix has runs key when unhealthy. Also possible hole: mutant results all-red in a single-probe run and no `runs` key — the code's healthy check for len(results)<2 allows all-red; if `runs` absent (healthy), that's a PASS from a run that might have timed out—but current probe_matrix writes runs if timeout, so not possible unless matrix hand-edited/cached. The workflow uses fresh matrix each run. So for the *current* live path, PASS without proof? Let me look for other holes:

1. `_decide_case` for judged: `judged["cases"] = _case_entries(...)`, plant_found true; if a judged case's entry has holds true + evidence → PASS. The plant check only verifies the *judge* found the plant; it doesn't verify the judge's PASS is right. That's by design (E9's plant), but an over-strict checklist could pass a broken product → false PASS. K1d had no judged cases at all; K1e none. So the judged path has never run live on a non-plant case? Report says K1d "no judged cases this time (judges skipped)". Calibration on INDEX.md had maybe 1 judged case in K1b? Backlog 12: "on no product with 2 or more judged cases. So the plant check was exercised with one judged case, or none." That's a coverage gap.

2. `static` cases get probes that are pytest tests. Fine.

3. Vision lines: EV-R1.2 parses vision "never" and "how we know". But the requirements file's VISION.md for INDEX.md presumably has these sections. Good.

4. Spec parsing: requirement ids that don't follow format silently unparsed? F14 adds unparsed_ids; meets false. Good.

5. Open ids: out of scope. If all ids open → requirement_rows empty → meets false (F1). Good.

6. `false FAIL` risk: confirms only one quote check + model faithful; still possible. K2 miss was over-specified probe → UNKNOWN not FAIL. Not a PASS.

7. "can a crash or timeout read as anything but UNKNOWN?" — a step crash blocks the run (no verdict) rather than UNKNOWN per requirement. The workflow returns blocked. If wired naively to accept `meets` only, blocked ≠ pass; but the guard needs an explicit failure record for blocked runs? R12.1 says evaluator reports fail — never pass — when result missing/empty/wrong form or evaluator errors/times out. The evaluator *blocks* rather than reports FAIL. That's a gap with R12.1 if the wiring treats "blocked" as non-failure. Also the workflow returns `status: blocked` with no verdict; the guard's failure model has no "could not run" state. This maps to memory-check lesson: five result states needed (pass, fail, needs a reviewer, not applicable, could not run); the evaluator has PASS, FAIL, UNKNOWN (a catch-all) + blocked at run level. So "could not run" is not a per-case state, and a run-level block isn't a result.

8. Timeout in `verdict.py`: no timeout concept; if verdict.py hangs? Not relevant.

9. Can a timeout read as PASS? mutant timeout → runs status timeout → UNKNOWN. Product timeout → all probes red → confirm; confirm-input needs output; no output → voided → UNKNOWN. However what if a product timeout happens and `matrix["output"]` is absent, but confirm-input still writes entries with `output`: ""? Let me check confirm-input code around line 140-220 to see if entries with no output are skipped or included with empty output. DATA says confirm-check voids "no failure output". Good.

10. Another interesting hole: if the product run *passes* all probes, but the mutant run is fine; PASS. But if the product run has `product_changed` false but a probe passes for the wrong reason? Mutant proves the probe can fail *on a broken version*, not that it tests the requirement; confirm only runs on red probes, not on passes. So a probe that is green on product and red on mutant can still be testing something else — the code cannot prove fidelity of green probes. E8 only confirms red ones. This is the fundamental proof gap: PASS means "probe green + red on a mutant", and the mutant was written by a model to match the case. If both probe and mutant encode the same misreading of the requirement, the case PASSes while the requirement isn't met. The E8 confirmation applies only to FAILs. That's a real "PASS without proof" in the sense of proof *of fidelity*; vision says "A PASS needs a check that passes on the product and was shown to fail on a broken copy" — which the code does. So by the vision's own definition, PASS has the proof it names. But not proof against the requirement sentence. That's worth flagging as a limit, not a violation. The vision's own wording is weaker than the intent.

Also: the mutant writer sees `classify.json` and workspace (code+spec), not probes. So writing a mutant for the case can be gamed by misunderstanding; but that's design.

11. Another PASS hole: a case classified `static`? Same mechanism.

12. `EV-R1.2` vision: only bullets under "What it will never do"/"How we know it works". The INDEX.md VISION has "How we know it works" lines; but this evaluator's own VISION's "How we know it works" is marked [proposed; measured in D5]. Fine.

13. Evaluator doesn't read use cases. R12.2 says vision, use cases, requirements. No EV requirement covers use cases. This is the biggest scope block for "judge of every build": the framework's use cases live in `docs/use-cases...`? Let me check the repo for use cases file and HARNESS_REQUIREMENTS mention. Brief says "Its inputs (it reads vision and requirements — not use cases)". So state as a fact from the snapshot: EV-R1.1/R1.2 only REQUIREMENTS and VISION; no use case input in EV-R3.1 args. The evaluator can't judge a failure against use cases. But is a use case check needed? R12.2's failure definition includes use cases. If the evaluator is only checking requirements, then a failure against a use case not in REQUIREMENTS would not be found by it. So it cannot be the judge of *every* build under R12.2 unless use cases are folded into REQUIREMENTS (VISION might have them?). This is a top item.

14. Cost: ~0.8-1.2M subagent tokens and 20+ min per evaluation (K1d total ~1.24M, 24+12 agents, ~18+ min legs). If every build is judged, per-build cost and wall time. Also 5 model families; Codex usage limit risk (seen twice). For "every build" this is a big cost; need tiering/batching. P3 open.

15. Result states: PASS/FAIL/UNKNOWN + blocked. Does "UNKNOWN" distinguish "needs EJ" (human), "unproven", "no probe", "not judged"? Yes reason strings, but no machine-readable category. Output format verdict.json cases have `reason`; consumers need to parse prose. The memory-check lesson says five states needed: fail, pass, needs a reviewer, not applicable, could not run. The evaluator collapses: needs reviewer → UNKNOWN "needs EJ" (string), not applicable → UNKNOWN, could not run → blocked run or UNKNOWN. So guard cannot distinguish "not applicable" (no failure) from "could not run" (a harness failure) without string matching. That matters for using it as the judge of every build: every UNKNOWN makes MEETS false, so a product with a human-only requirement can never MEET. That's a design decision to revisit: MEETS requires every in-scope requirement PASS (EV-P1.2); human cases never PASS, so any spec with a human-judged case can never MEET, even if correct. Hmm — EV-C1.1 "human: only EJ can tell" → verdict.py human → UNKNOWN always. So any product with a human case can never pass overall. Is that intended? It follows EV-P1.2. For use as the judge of every build, that's a blocker: many products will have human-only requirements, so MEETS: no forever. Not necessarily a bug; EJ decides. Still a fact.

16. Output format: verdict.json top-level has `meets`, `counts`, `cases`, `requirements`, `out_of_scope`, `manifest`, `input_problems`, `orphan_probes`, maybe `meets_reason`. Lacks: product path, spec path, commit/hash identity, engine/model per step, timestamps, run start/end (cost.json separate), and a machine-readable `status` (blocked runs write no verdict.json at all). For guard wiring: `fail --found-by evaluator` needs --check (could be requirement id), --input (not recorded), --expected (requirement sentence — not in verdict.json; requires group text from requirements.json? requirements.json has fails_if and cases text, but verdict.json doesn't include case text), --actual (probe output — in matrix.json output but not in verdict.json FAIL rows; only repro), --engine/--harness (not recorded per case), --reason (verdict reason), --evidence (must point to path). So a wiring script must join verdict.json + requirements.json + matrix.json + cost.json + the original args. Args aren't recorded anywhere. That's a concrete blocker to wiring: the run is not self-describing. Also the guard's `fail` requires --evidence non-empty; verdict.json FAIL has repro but evidence path could be run folder. Fine.

