A more subtle PASS hole: `runs` is written only when some run is not healthy. Suppose a mutant run has a *collection error* that makes *all* probes fail and status "ok" with errors list; healthy false due to red in errors check → UNKNOWN. Good. Suppose an *all-red run* with JUnit failure (not error) and 2+ probes: healthy requires any outcome != fail → false → UNKNOWN. Good. Single-probe all-red run: len<2 passes (c), and `runs` absent because status ok, no errors → counts as proof → PASS. That's F5(c) by design. Can a single-probe mutant run be all-red for a reason other than the mutant (e.g. the probe fails for a wrong reason on product? No—product must be green for PAAS; if product green and mutant all-red, then the mutant caused the red... unless the mutant breaks imports or syntax, in which case the red isn't proof of the case). A syntax-breaking mutant is still "broken copy", but the probe red may be due to syntax error, not the requirement. That's still evidence the probe detects a break, albeit not fidelity. This is the E8-only-for-FAIL asymmetry. So PASS can exist without proof of fidelity to the sentence. But the vision's definition is met. I should carefully phrase.

